{% block body %}
<article class="article-shell">
  <header class="article-header"><p class="article-kicker">REVERSE PROXIES · 9 MIN READ · AUGUST 28, 2026</p><h1>Nginx to Caddy Migration</h1><p class="article-dek">Common rewrite rules, real Caddyfile equivalents, and the path and header traps that only show up after deployment.</p><div class="article-header-meta"><span>StackHal Field Notes</span><span>Migration guide</span></div></header>
  <div class="article-layout"><aside class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><span>ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#mental-model">The mental model</a><a href="#locations">Locations</a><a href="#proxy">proxy_pass</a><a href="#redirects">Redirects and headers</a><a href="#prefixes">Path prefixes</a><a href="#checklist">Migration checklist</a></aside>
    <div class="article-content"><p id="mental-model" class="article-lead">Caddy is not a line-for-line Nginx replacement. The successful migration is a mapping exercise: identify the request matcher, the handler, and the response behavior in each Nginx block, then express those three things in Caddy’s directive order.</p>
      <div class="article-callout article-callout-accent"><strong>The useful mental model</strong><span>Nginx chooses a location and then runs directives inside it. Caddy builds a handler chain from matchers and directives. Similar-looking configuration can therefore have different ordering and path semantics.</span></div>
      <figure class="article-visual visual-before-after"><div><b>BEFORE · NGINX</b><pre>location /api/ {
  proxy_pass http://backend:8080/;
}</pre></div><div class="before-after-arrow">→</div><div><b>AFTER · CADDY</b><pre>handle /api/* {
  reverse_proxy backend:8080
}</pre></div><figcaption>Move the intent, not the punctuation: match the path, then proxy it.</figcaption></figure>
      <h2 id="locations">1. Translate location blocks into explicit matchers</h2><p>A prefix location such as <code>location /assets/</code> becomes a path matcher. A broad <code>location /</code> is usually a fallback handler. Keep the narrow routes explicit so a later edit cannot accidentally swallow them.</p><pre class="article-code"><code># Nginx
location /assets/ { try_files $uri =404; }
location / { proxy_pass http://app:3000; }

# Caddy
@assets path /assets/*
handle @assets { file_server }
handle { reverse_proxy app:3000 }</code></pre>
      <p>Use <code>handle</code> when only one matching handler should win. Use <code>route</code> when you need a deliberately ordered group of handlers. That distinction is one of the safest ways to avoid accidental fall-through.</p>
      <h2 id="proxy">2. proxy_pass is where paths quietly change</h2><p>In Nginx, the trailing slash on <code>proxy_pass</code> controls whether the matched location prefix is replaced. Caddy’s <code>reverse_proxy</code> forwards the request path by default. If the upstream expects a different path, rewrite it explicitly.</p><pre class="article-code"><code># Nginx: /api/users becomes /users upstream
location /api/ { proxy_pass http://backend:8080/; }

# Caddy: make the replacement visible
handle /api/* {
  uri strip_prefix /api
  reverse_proxy backend:8080
}</code></pre>
      <div class="article-screen screen-transpiler" role="img" aria-label="Illustration of StackHal Caddyfile Transpiler comparing Nginx and Caddy configuration"><div class="screen-chrome"><span class="screen-dot"></span><span class="screen-dot"></span><span class="screen-dot"></span><span>stackhal.com / caddy-transpiler</span></div><div class="transpiler-body"><div><small>NGINX INPUT</small><code>location /api/ { proxy_pass http://api:8080/; }</code><code>add_header X-Trace edge;</code></div><div class="transpiler-arrow">→</div><div><small>CADDYFILE OUTPUT</small><code>handle /api/* {</code><code>  uri strip_prefix /api</code><code>  reverse_proxy api:8080</code><code>}</code></div></div><span class="screen-caption">Use Caddyfile Transpiler to get a first draft, then review path intent and ordering.</span></div>
      <h2 id="redirects">3. Redirects and headers need their own review</h2><p>Translate permanent and temporary redirects with <code>redir</code>. For a regex redirect, use a named matcher so the capture behavior is visible. Headers set on the response belong in <code>header</code>; headers sent to the upstream belong in <code>header_up</code>.</p><pre class="article-code"><code># Nginx
return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
proxy_set_header Host $host;
add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600";

# Caddy
redir https://{host}{uri} permanent
reverse_proxy app:3000 {
  header_up Host {host}
}
header Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600"</code></pre>
      <p>Do not copy <code>add_header</code> as a proxy request header. It changes what the browser receives, while <code>header_up</code> changes what the application receives. Check both sides with a real request.</p>
      <h2 id="prefixes">4. Path prefixes, files, and try_files</h2><p>Nginx often combines <code>try_files</code>, a fallback URI, and a proxy in one location. Caddy’s file server and handlers make that split clearer, but you must decide whether a missing file should become a 404 or continue to the application.</p><pre class="article-code"><code># Static files first, application second
@static path /assets/* /favicon.svg
handle @static {
  root * /srv/site/public
  file_server
}
handle {
  reverse_proxy app:3000
}</code></pre>
      <h2 id="checklist">A migration checklist that catches the traps</h2><ol class="article-checklist"><li><strong>Validate:</strong> run <code>caddy validate --config Caddyfile</code> before reload.</li><li><strong>Compare paths:</strong> test a prefixed route and confirm the upstream sees the intended URI.</li><li><strong>Compare redirects:</strong> check status, Location, scheme, host, and query-string preservation.</li><li><strong>Compare headers:</strong> inspect both browser response headers and upstream request logs.</li><li><strong>Check fallbacks:</strong> test missing files, slash variants, OPTIONS, and 404 responses.</li><li><strong>Keep rollback simple:</strong> retain the working Nginx config until representative traffic is green.</li></ol>
      <div class="article-cta"><p class="article-kicker">READY TO TRANSLATE?</p><h2>Start with a readable first draft.</h2><p>Paste an Nginx or Apache configuration into Caddyfile Transpiler, then review the generated matchers, path handling, and headers.</p><div class="article-cta-actions"><a class="article-button article-button-primary" href="{{ path('caddy_transpiler') }}">Open Caddyfile Transpiler →</a><a class="article-button" href="{{ path('blog_index') }}">Back to the blog</a></div></div>
